 //
 // Halo 3 measures real time in vblanks. main_time_update (82088640) waits for
 // at least one vblank since the previous frame, then returns
-// dt = vblanks elapsed / refresh rate. The renderer's present (821924C0) then
-// waits until two vblanks after the frame started, which is what holds the
-// game at 30 FPS on a 60 Hz display. The simulation itself is driven by real
-// elapsed time: game_time_update (820983E0) converts dt into whole 30 Hz ticks
-// and carries the fraction in game_time_globals+0x14.
+// dt = vblanks elapsed / refresh rate. The present (821924C0) then waits for
+// the frame's target vblank. The simulation itself is driven by real elapsed
+// time: game_time_update (820983E0) converts dt into whole 30 Hz ticks and
+// carries the fraction in game_time_globals+0x14.
 //
-// Two more gates hold gameplay at 30 / 60 FPS: the outer main loop
-// (sub_821A9790) waits for a vblank whenever none has happened since its
-// previous pass, and a pass only renders if the frame has a reason to
-// (TLS[0xD0] flags); in a game that is only when a tick ran.
+// In game the work is split over two threads. The main thread runs the main
+// loop (sub_821A9790): input, the simulation and the camera, then it
+// publishes the frame, a copy of what the renderer needs (the object pool
+// among it), to a queue. The RENDER thread (sub_821A8500) takes frames from
+// the queue, draws them from the copy and presents. The mode word 82948F68 is
+// 1 while it does that; 0 means single-threaded, the main thread drawing each
+// frame itself, which the game only switches to briefly (some loads).
 //
-// To unlock the frame rate we skip the three vblank waits, render on every
-// pass, and feed main_time_update's result from the host clock instead of the
-// vblank counter, so dt stays exact at any frame rate and the simulation
-// speed is unchanged. The frame-rate cap is a host-side wait at the start of
-// the frame, before input is read. Loading screens, drawn by the render
-// thread, keep the original pacing. Everything is decided per frame from
-// cvars, because the per-game config is only loaded after the title module
-// (and these hooks) are set up.
-
+// Three more gates hold the game at 30 FPS: the main loop waits for a vblank
+// whenever none has happened since its previous pass, so it runs at most at
+// 60 Hz, and a pass only publishes a frame if it has a reason to (TLS[0xD0]
+// flags), which in a game is only when a tick ran.
+//
+// To unlock the frame rate we skip the three vblank waits, publish a frame on
+// every pass, and feed main_time_update's result from the host clock instead
+// of the vblank counter, so dt stays exact at any frame rate and the
+// simulation speed is unchanged. The frame-rate cap is a host-side wait at the
+// start of the main loop body, before input is read; the render thread draws
+// as fast as frames are published. Presents outside the main loop (loading
+// screens drawn while the main thread loads) are already spaced out by the
+// code that triggers them. Everything is decided per frame from cvars,
+// because the per-game config is only loaded after the title module (and
+// these hooks) are set up.
